assertion  XSH.fma.001
anchor     functions/fma.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fma.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       fma(2, 3, 4), (-2, 3, 1), (0.5, 0.5, 0.25), (1.5, 2, -3), (0, 5, 2)
           and (3, 0.25, 0.25) give 10, -5, 0.5, 0, 2 and 1.
test       t/001-values.c

assertion  XSH.fma.002
anchor     functions/fma.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fma.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       For x = y = 1 + epsilon and z the negation of the product x*y after it
           is rounded to the type, fma(x, y, z) is not zero (the product error
           survives), and when the rounding direction is to nearest it equals
           epsilon squared.
note       Q-108: the rounding direction is taken from fegetround(), not from
           FLT_ROUNDS; the equality with epsilon squared is asserted only when
           fegetround() reports to-nearest.
test       t/002-single-rounding.c

assertion  XSH.fma.003
anchor     functions/fma.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fma.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       fma(NaN, 2, 3), (2, NaN, 3), (NaN, NaN, 3), (NaN, 2, NaN), (2, 3, NaN)
           and (Inf, 2, NaN) return a NaN.
test       t/003-nan.c

assertion  XSH.fma.004
anchor     functions/fma.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fma.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/fma.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      2001
says       fma(Inf, 0, 1), (0, Inf, 1), (-Inf, 0, 1), (0, -Inf, Inf) and (Inf, 0,
           0) return a NaN and are domain errors, reported through errno (EDOM)
           and the invalid exception as math_errhandling selects.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/004-inf-times-zero.c

assertion  XSH.fma.005
anchor     functions/fma.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fma.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/fma.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      2001
says       fma(Inf, 1, -Inf), (-Inf, 1, Inf), (1, Inf, -Inf), (Inf, Inf, -Inf)
           and (Inf, -1, Inf) return a NaN and are domain errors, reported as
           math_errhandling selects.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/005-inf-minus-inf.c

assertion  XSH.fma.006
anchor     functions/fma.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fma.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       fma(Inf, 0, NaN) and fma(0, Inf, NaN) return a NaN (whether a domain
           error occurs is left open).
test       t/006-inf-zero-nan.c

assertion  XSH.fma.007
anchor     functions/fma.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fma.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/fma.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      2001
says       fma(MAX, 2, 0) and fma(-MAX, 2, 0), with MAX the largest finite value
           of the type, overflow: the result is HUGE_VAL (negated for the second)
           and the range error is reported through errno (ERANGE) and the
           overflow exception as math_errhandling selects.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard). The value HUGE_VAL comes from the general rule for
           overflow (XBD 4.23.3.1).
test       t/007-overflow.c

assertion  XSH.fma.008
anchor     functions/fma.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fma.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.23
class      required
since      2001
says       With errno holding EDOM before the call, fma(2, 3, 4) leaves it
           unchanged and, when exception flags are in use, raises none of the
           invalid, divide-by-zero and overflow exceptions.
test       t/008-no-error.c
